3955	215d0f3c-e697-401c-bffa-bce58e2b9278	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Missing Anti-clickjacking Header	Modern Web browsers support the Content-Security-Policy and X-Frame-Options HTTP headers. Ensure one of them is set on all web pages returned by your site/app.\nIf you expect the page to be framed only by pages on your server (e.g. it's part of a FRAMESET) then you'll want to use SAMEORIGIN, otherwise if you never expect the page to be framed, you should use DENY. Alternatively consider implementing Content Security Policy's "frame-ancestors" directive.	The response does not protect against 'ClickJacking' attacks. It should include either Content-Security-Policy with 'frame-ancestors' directive or X-Frame-Options.	warning	Medium	\N	No		Open	18bf718383bafa2c79a7dc2d206433da3cd0efe2545784e4afb8b810e6cbc6b8	No	\N	Zap		https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/X-Frame-Options	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.554685+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.554715+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3956	c3b98623-33ac-4570-ba6f-3527129d5890	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Content Security Policy (CSP) Header Not Set	Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to set the Content-Security-Policy header.	Content Security Policy (CSP) is an added layer of security that helps to detect and mitigate certain types of attacks, including Cross Site Scripting (XSS) and data injection attacks. These attacks are used for everything from data theft to site defacement or distribution of malware. CSP provides a set of standard HTTP headers that allow website owners to declare approved sources of content that browsers should be allowed to load on that page — covered types are JavaScript, CSS, HTML frames, fonts, images and embeddable objects such as Java applets, ActiveX, audio and video files.	warning	Medium	\N	No		Open	7b823bb50e8ed291d77067efa964ab984e861b4a293e3420ce3873caacf28bf8	No	\N	Zap		https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP\nhttps://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/CSP/\nhttps://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/\nhttps://web.dev/articles/csp\nhttps://caniuse.com/#feat=contentsecuritypolicy\nhttps://content-security-policy.com/	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.569652+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.569682+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3957	6165e93f-e485-4328-b705-616686b4e885	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Server Leaks Version Information via "Server" HTTP Response Header Field	Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress the "Server" header or provide generic details.	The web/application server is leaking version information via the "Server" HTTP response header. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other vulnerabilities your web/application server is subject to.	info	Low	\N	No		Open	d68606412b7709fa53b6078db941d59991f3a4596a4f0608c5433f1d3b9e86b9	No	\N	Zap	Apache/2.2.8 (Ubuntu) DAV/2	https://httpd.apache.org/docs/current/mod/core.html#servertokens\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/msp-n-p/ff648552(v=pandp.10)\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.586102+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.586136+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3958	3fa12c9a-af65-4f51-935d-e0d78ea10532	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	X-Content-Type-Options Header Missing	Ensure that the application/web server sets the Content-Type header appropriately, and that it sets the X-Content-Type-Options header to 'nosniff' for all web pages.\nIf possible, ensure that the end user uses a standards-compliant and modern web browser that does not perform MIME-sniffing at all, or that can be directed by the web application/web server to not perform MIME-sniffing.	The Anti-MIME-Sniffing header X-Content-Type-Options was not set to 'nosniff'. This allows older versions of Internet Explorer and Chrome to perform MIME-sniffing on the response body, potentially causing the response body to be interpreted and displayed as a content type other than the declared content type. Current (early 2014) and legacy versions of Firefox will use the declared content type (if one is set), rather than performing MIME-sniffing.	info	Low	\N	No		Open	24c9634cf1a21c15dee8f7d18ef14382ec845a8815f7e45793d461be79743504	No	\N	Zap		https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/internet-explorer/ie-developer/compatibility/gg622941(v=vs.85)\nhttps://owasp.org/www-community/Security_Headers	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.613504+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.613532+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3959	85d924cd-e660-45bd-a89f-c513cff762cc	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Server Leaks Information via "X-Powered-By" HTTP Response Header Field(s)	Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress "X-Powered-By" headers.	The web/application server is leaking information via one or more "X-Powered-By" HTTP response headers. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other frameworks/components your web application is reliant upon and the vulnerabilities such components may be subject to.	info	Low	\N	No		Open	85f4ca9469622cf10f558359f5bc65b06e048bdb4bc4364963402ab2a6b8052e	No	\N	Zap	X-Powered-By: PHP/5.2.4-2ubuntu5.10	https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/v42/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/08-Fingerprint_Web_Application_Framework\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.63184+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.631858+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3960	0de3451f-facb-4333-933e-aaa0f5512bbd	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Application Error Disclosure	Review the source code of this page. Implement custom error pages. Consider implementing a mechanism to provide a unique error reference/identifier to the client (browser) while logging the details on the server side and not exposing them to the user.	This page contains an error/warning message that may disclose sensitive information like the location of the file that produced the unhandled exception. This information can be used to launch further attacks against the web application. The alert could be a false positive if the error message is found inside a documentation page.	warning	Medium	\N	No		Open	2c430a0ea3d3234a3e219e1b52b39adebaa346b375caa39c717845bd19e558ff	No	\N	Zap	Parent Directory		1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.6439+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.643938+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3961	e4edc7df-91c0-4c36-bc7d-a489e2b00b4d	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Directory Browsing	Configure the web server to disable directory browsing.	It is possible to view a listing of the directory contents. Directory listings may reveal hidden scripts, include files, backup source files, etc., which can be accessed to reveal sensitive information.	warning	Medium	\N	No		Open	1dffa881a953c0acf504a73fdee4bfd7a4d863505ae48b80bd33f40896faf24e	No	\N	Zap	<title>Index of /dav</title>	https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.660943+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.66096+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
3962	ab3eab85-2ab3-4aa5-b793-f192f9fea19f	32cbb938-424c-434c-abcd-a783b73cf694	\N	http://172.18.0.10/	Cookie without SameSite Attribute	Ensure that the SameSite attribute is set to either 'lax' or ideally 'strict' for all cookies.	A cookie has been set without the SameSite attribute, which means that the cookie can be sent as a result of a 'cross-site' request. The SameSite attribute is an effective counter measure to cross-site request forgery, cross-site script inclusion, and timing attacks.	info	Low	\N	No		Open	aab946c145568d779bd8bfa4e8801702213fe2017f7421c9dde81b10b477b256	No	\N	Zap	Set-Cookie: phpMyAdmin	https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-httpbis-cookie-same-site	1	2026-09-12 13:17:24.69348+00	\N	\N	2026-09-12 13:17:24.693524+00	t	1	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
